README tetris

OVERVIEW:
    Tetris has 5 Java Classes (not including Constants, App, or CpuTimeTracker),
    and they interact in the following ways:
        - PaneOrganizer manages and adds/removes all graphical elements of the screen.
        PaneOrganizer instantiates Game in it's constructor. It also passes gamePane
        into the game, which allows elements to be added without give game access
        to PaneOrganizer.
        - Game is the top level class that controls the maine game logic. This means it
        detects all key inputs, sets up and handles the timeline, generates the pieces. It
        is also in charge of creating labels and handling pause/gameOver logic. When the game
        is paused, the timeline is also paused.
        Additionally, game handles restart logic an updates the difficulty of the game depending
        on score.
        - The board class manages all logic related to the board and the pieces as a system.
        This creates the distinction between board and piece, as piece is solely responsible
        for individual piece logic. Apart from creating the actual board, board hold the
        important isOccupied 2D array, this array stores every block from every piece in an
        occupied space, which is important for collision, line clearing, and piece movement.
        The board also has several nested for-loops that are responsible for line clearing
        and moving blocks above the cleared line down.
        - The piece class is responsible for every newly generated piece's logic.
        This includes movement based on key inputs: horizontal, rotational, and instant
        drop, as well as, the constant falling mechanic called by the timeline's keyframe
        duration. As a result of this, piece knows of Game. Most of this logic is controlled
        by collision, so piece also knows of board.
        - Block is what every piece is made up of. Each block is the same square created
        using a javaFX rectangle. This makes block a wrapper class that enhances the
        rectangle's customization, as well as serving as a buffer between tetris classes
        and javaFX classes. Each block has several getters and setters to maintain its
        wrapper nature, and is made to glow using javaFX effects.

DESIGN CHOICES:
        - Logically it would make sense for Board to be in charge of piece generation.
        However, because game needs to have the key input switch and the timeline, this
        would cause board to have several small and repetitive "carry-on" methods that
        essentially call the same exact method in piece serving as a sort of bridge
        between game and piece. This is bad design, so piece generation was moved directly
        to Game.
        - Board and Piece have similar logic, however, to maintain their functions separate
        and clean, board solely focuses on whole group logic dealing with occupied spaces,
        line clearing, and border generation, while piece on focused on movement and
        single-piece specific things.
        - To make my game look different from other tetris games, I made the border red
        (which fits the accessibility metrics) as well as removed stroke. I then added
        a glow using the dropShadow javaFX effect which makes the pieces look neon.
        - To make the labels for score, level, and difficulty show up on top of the board,
        I made a special pane for them in game using an HBox. PaneOrganizer is responsible
        for all top-level graphical elements, but to maintain Game's lack of knowledge of
        paneOrganizer, this pane was essential for making the labels appear.
        - Because a lot of logic in piece is repeated, such as horizontal movement and
        checking for next piece occupancy, several initial methods were combined into
        one. Instead of having two separate methods for x and y movement, I made one
        that takes in a direction variable in the form of an int. And since the dropping
        down mechanic and the horizontal movement both check for block occupancy in similar
        ways, I made a method in board that accomplishes that and returns a boolean.

EXTRA CREDIT:
        - Restart: The game can be restarted at anytime. This was difficult to achieve
        because sometimes pieces would stack over each other and leave permanent blocks
        on the board. I fixed this by creating a new board everytime the game was restarted
        removing all pieces and blocks completely.
        - Progressive Difficulty: Line clearing now determines your level, which is
        displayed on the screen. The higher the level, the lower the duration of each
        keyframe in the timeline, making the pieces fall faster and making the game
        more difficult. Using Math.max, the speed caps out at a duration of 0.15 seconds
        per keyframe.
        - Manual Difficulty: Aside from progressive difficulty, the player can also
        manually set the difficulty of the game to be easy, medium, or hard. Each setting
        corresponds with 1 2 3 key input. This mechanic works in tandem with progressive
        difficulty.
        - Lines Cleared: The Score, level, and lines cleared are tracked. Lines cleared
        specifically shows up on the gameOver screen.
        - Energy Consumption: (See note below)

SRC:
        - Because the game uses 7 different pieces, each color needs to  contrast with each
        other while passing the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1. To achieve
        this I chose some unique colors and plugged each of them into https://contrast-ratio.org/
        I did this until all my colors passed the ratio test while also contrasting with each
        other.

SRC ENERGY CONSUMPTION:
        - CPU Time: 2.938 seconds
          Estimated Energy Usage: 132.188 watt seconds/joules
        - CPU Time: 2.188 seconds
          Estimated Energy Usage: 98.438 watt seconds/joules
        - CPU Time: 1.578 seconds
          Estimated Energy Usage: 71.016 watt seconds/joules
        - Average Consumption: About 70.075 J
        - A smartphone charging draws about 5 W, so this energy could charge it for
         about 14 seconds. A Laptop running is about 60 W so it could run one for around 2
         seconds. An LED light bulb could produce 7 seconds of light because its power
         is about 10 W.

